rizontal axes. In example 1, the first shape is symmetric around row 4 and the second shape about row 11. Now, looking at example 2, I'm focusing on a shape with a vertical axis of symmetry, at column 3.5. It seems this symmetry principle is crucial for the transformation.\n\n\n**Confirming Symmetry Types**\n\nI'm now investigating symmetry types, noticing the shift from horizontal to vertical axes. It seems each shape might have an inherent best axis for reconstruction, vertical or horizontal. I'm re-examining the examples to determine which axis best defines each shape, in order to identify a pattern, specifically looking at if horizontal symmetries are preserved, or if they are transformed into vertical symmetries and vice-versa.\n\n\n**Reflecting Shape Components**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a reflection-based transformation. I've noted that horizontally symmetric shapes are reflected across their axis. Noise appears to overwrite shape pixels, but the original structure determines the symmetry. It seems like reflecting each connected color component, and taking the union, or majority, of the shape and its reflection, reconstructs the transformed shape.\n\n\n",
